WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.859
JSON is a data format, not an authorization mechanism and not a business
rule engine.

2
00:00:04.859 --> 00:00:11.428
A JSON document can represent strings, numbers, booleans, null values,
arrays, objects, and nested structures.

3
00:00:11.428 --> 00:00:13.892
That structure still needs expectations.

4
00:00:13.892 --> 00:00:22.514
The application should know which fields are required, which fields are
optional, which fields are allowed, and what each value is permitted to
mean.

5
00:00:22.514 --> 00:00:23.814
Shape and type matter.

6
00:00:23.814 --> 00:00:27.509
A value that should be a list should not silently become a string.

7
00:00:27.509 --> 00:00:32.779
A field that should contain a small positive integer should not accept an
arbitrary number.

8
00:00:32.779 --> 00:00:38.595
Numeric range and precision assumptions can affect identifiers, money,
counters, and timestamps.

9
00:00:38.595 --> 00:00:45.712
Character encoding expectations also matter when data moves between
clients, logs, databases, and downstream services.

10
00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:49.818
JSON processing should include size limits and nesting expectations.

11
00:00:49.818 --> 00:00:57.072
Large payloads, deeply nested objects, or repeated structures can consume
memory, CPU, and time before business logic runs.

12
00:00:57.072 --> 00:01:00.562
Some applications should reject oversized requests early.

13
00:01:00.562 --> 00:01:05.420
Others may need streaming or incremental processing for intentionally
large data.

14
00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:10.074
The key is to choose deliberately rather than allowing any structure by
default.

15
00:01:10.074 --> 00:01:13.564
After parsing, validate the schema and the business intent.

16
00:01:13.564 --> 00:01:20.749
Duplicate keys, unknown fields, extra nesting, surprising nulls, and
unexpected arrays can all lead to confused behavior.

17
00:01:20.749 --> 00:01:28.139
Rejecting unknown fields may be appropriate for strict APIs, while
versioned compatibility may require a different strategy.

18
00:01:28.139 --> 00:01:32.040
Either way, the code should make the decision visible and testable.
